WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.799 --> 00:00:06.799
Hello.

00:00:06.799 --> 00:00:11.015
It’s our very first episode of Journey to
the Microcosmos,

00:00:11.015 --> 00:00:12.760
and to start, we thought

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:14.440
some introductions were in order.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:15.620
I’m Hank Green.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:18.960
I make YouTube channels,
including SciShow and Crash Course,

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.000
and this is James.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.100
James is a scientist, living in Europe,
who has, what I think,

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:26.920
is the appropriate amount

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:29.680
of enthusiasm for microscopic life,

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:32.399
which is to say, a lot of it.

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:36.160
James has active, living colonies of microorganisms

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:37.420
throughout his home.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:39.250
Though, for clarity,

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:40.260
we all do.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.440
He just has them intentionally.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:45.020
He keeps tardigrades fed and happy.

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:48.960
Gathers samples to search out new organisms for his collection

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.519
and even takes samples in from elsewhere.

00:00:51.519 --> 00:00:53.900
The organisms we will explore on this channel

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.620
are going to be almost entirely aquatic.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:00.160
This makes them easier to care for and to observe.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:07.320
But, in the world of the micro, aquatic environments
might be tiny droplets of water in soil,

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:12.900
on treebark, or the thin film of water covering
the moss in your back yard.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:19.540
Organisms featured on this channel will be
collected everywhere from soils to ponds,

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:22.130
from puddles to seas.

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:27.890
These ecosystems are similar to any you might
find in the macroscopic world, in many ways.

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:31.640
Tiny organisms converting light into energy, like plants.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.030
Tiny organisms eating those organisms.

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:37.260
And other tiny organisms eating those organisms.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:42.700
Though, on the African Savannah, you will rarely see a zebra consume a tree whole,

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:48.060
and then allow it to continue photosynthesizing through
its transparent belly

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:51.220
in order to produce sustenance for them both.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:57.060
Also, you very rarely see a tree eat a zebra in our world.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:01.700
But as we will see, the Microcosmos can be
a bit strange at times.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:07.000
James keeps what are called “cultures”
alive and thriving in his home.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:12.700
These cultures are whole ecosystems with many
different species living alongside each other.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:18.880
One is a monoculture of harmless algae that
were living on blueberries from the supermarket.

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:23.640
And we’ll soon be getting samples from
one of the last remaining untouched forests

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:27.260
of Europe and from the Permafrost of Siberia.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:36.140
While this channel is going to explore remarkable diversity,

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:43.280
we think it’s important to start out with the three main sorts of organisms you're gonna see here.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.740
First, and most simple, the prokaryotes.

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:54.720
Single-celled and lacking organelles, the
ones you’re most familiar with are bacteria.

00:02:54.730 --> 00:03:02.120
For hundreds of millions, maybe even billions
of years, prokaryotes were the only life on our planet.

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:07.600
And, for clarity, the earth is only four and
a half billion years old.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:13.540
The main thing that distinguishes them from
the Eukaryotes is that they don’t have membrane-bound

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:18.700
interior structures like the nucleus or chloroplasts or mitochondria.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:24.880
But since they have been around for so long,
prokaryotes are remarkable in their diversity.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:32.810
They can be huge or tiny, a magnificent variety
of shapes and colors from green to red to purple.

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:35.960
Some contain mysterious crystals in their cells.

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:42.770
Some reproduce by being broken into pieces,
a handy trait when there are lots of things biting at you.

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:44.790
And some of them can really move!

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:50.480
With the aid of flagella, long fibrous proteins
that extend through the cell wall and wiggle around,

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:55.090
some bacteria are the cheetahs of
the microcosmos.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:06.940
The tend to be tiny, bacteria aren’t always
easy to see even at 600x magnification.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:10.400
Though some can be comparatively huge.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:16.870
And that includes cyanobacteria, that, around
2.5 billion years ago, were the first organisms

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:22.220
to evolve the ability to turn sunlight into
chemical energy.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:24.759
That changed the game in a lot of ways.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:32.800
It was very good for many organisms, including,
eventually, us, and very bad for many others...but

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:34.480
that is a story for another day.

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:40.980
By 1.7 billion years ago, the second sort
of organism, you can expect to see here arrived...

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.270
the Eukaryotes.

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:47.590
Now, we don’t want to sound mean to our
prokayrote friends, who are massively varried

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:49.620
and complex and wonderful.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:53.080
But single-celled eukaryotes take it to another
level.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:59.229
They tend to be much larger, and so we can get a better view of their beauty and abilities.

00:04:59.229 --> 00:05:04.889
Sometimes, these organisms are even big enough for us to see without microscopes.

00:05:04.889 --> 00:05:10.300
Single-celled eukaryotes are often sometimes called protists, and we have to make the

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:14.520
“single-celled” distinction here because there are
also multi-celled eukaryotes

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:20.340
which include a lot of different organisms including mushrooms and trees and jellyfish

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:21.540
and you.

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:27.360
But since single-celled Eukaryotes only have
one cell, they can’t have different cells

00:05:27.379 --> 00:05:29.939
that do different things like we do.

00:05:29.939 --> 00:05:34.569
Instead, they have different areas of their
cells specialize.

00:05:34.569 --> 00:05:40.889
They have sensory systems; they can feel mechanical
stimuli and react to it to avoid predators.

00:05:40.889 --> 00:05:47.099
The red spot in this protist is an eyespot
that has light-sensing structures beneath it.

00:05:47.099 --> 00:05:50.550
They can hunt actively or can avoid being eaten.

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:54.389
They can build “shells” for themselves,
and form colonies.

00:05:54.389 --> 00:05:57.210
Some even show simple learning behaviors.

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:00.660
The complexity of these single-celled organisms

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:04.040
sometimes makes you feel that they have a consciousness.

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:06.880
And we're not here to say that they don't.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:15.900
Some of the eukaryotes we show are going to be photosynthetic like diatoms,

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:17.800
which, on their own,

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:25.080
generate 20-50% of the oxygen produced on earth and
have cell walls made of glass.

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:31.220
The best guess we have for how eukaryotic cells formed is that a single-celled prokaryote

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:37.759
ate another prokaryote and then found that life was better for both of them if they survived together.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:43.700
Even today, the mitochondria in our bodies hold their own separate DNA...

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:46.520
a relic of that ancient event.

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:51.840
And finally, the third category of organism
we’ll be seeing a lot of on this channel...

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:53.960
the micro-animals.

00:06:53.969 --> 00:06:57.960
These are multi-celled eukaryotes just like
you and me.

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:01.080
Animals like hydra, which can theoretically
live forever.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:04.340
Tardigrades that can survive the vacuum of space.

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:11.200
Rotifers that have thousands of cells with
specialized muscles, intestines, even brains...kinda.

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:14.040
So they are in many ways, like us.

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:18.720
But they also have some traits that sound other-worldly.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:23.260
Tardigrades are hatched with the exact same number of cells in their body

00:07:23.260 --> 00:07:24.760
that they will always have,

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:30.949
and these tiny baby water bears then
only grow as their cells grow bigger.

00:07:30.949 --> 00:07:38.629
And yet, these animals are so small that sometimes
a single-celled organism can swallow them whole.

00:07:38.629 --> 00:07:40.700
Sorry little rotifer.

00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:46.580
It’s important to note that one cell can
be 1000 times smaller than another cell.

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:51.540
So a rotifer can have thousands of cells,
but still be dwarfed by one protist.

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:55.540
Because different magnifications are better
for observing different organisms,

00:07:55.540 --> 00:08:03.720
we will always have the magnification on screen, whether that’s 10x, 100x, or as much as 1000x magnification.

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:10.869
So, prokaryotes...the simplest organisms you will see here, no organelles, but still massively varied.

00:08:10.869 --> 00:08:13.340
Single-celled eukaryotes, or protists,

00:08:13.340 --> 00:08:15.569
more varied, more specialized.

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:21.720
And Micro-animals, with thousands or even tens of thousands of individual cells.

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:27.260
Those are the three main categories of the kinds of organisms you can expect to see here.

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:31.160
If you want to see more from our "Master of Microscopes", James Weiss,

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:33.660
check out Jam's Germs on Instagram,

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:38.420
and if you want to see more from us, that my friends, is what that subscribe button is for.

00:08:38.420 --> 00:08:45.490
Thank you for coming on this journey with us, as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

